BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Maurer School of Law
DTSTART:20270211T220000Z
DTEND:20270211T230000Z
LOCATION:Lowell E. Baier Hall - Classroom - 120
GEO:39.165074;-86.526424
SUMMARY:Environmental Justice: Past\, Present\, and (Near) Future
DESCRIPTION:This talk is part of Conservation Law Center's "Talking Conse
 rvation" lecture series. For more information\, visit www.talkingconserva
 tion.org.  Light refreshments will be served.Abstract:  When activists an
 d scholars speak of "environmental justice\," the first word people often
  hear is race. And for good reason – early concerns for environmental j
 ustice indeed focused on disproportionate impacts of environmental harm o
 n communities of color and low-income populations. But over time\, enviro
 nmental justice concerns have expanded well beyond race\, to include all 
 people who may be disproportionately impacted by environmental harm and t
 o consider broader understandings of harm\, to include cumulative impacts
  and impacts to cultural and subsistence resources. This presentation wil
 l explore what environmental justice can or should mean today\, against a
  backdrop of federal retrenchment\, yet full of hope for the future drawi
 ng upon lessons from the past.About Professor Villa:  Professor Cliff Vil
 la teaches\, writes\, and practices in the areas of constitutional rights
  and environmental law\, with particular expertise in environmental justi
 ce and disaster response. On leave from UNM\, Prof. Villa served in the B
 iden administration as political leadership for the U.S. EPA Office of La
 nd and Emergency Management. In that role\, based in Washington\, D.C.\, 
 he provided policy direction for national programs including Superfund cl
 eanup\, Brownfields revitalization\, and emergency response. Previously\,
  Villa served more than 20 years as an EPA attorney in Washington\, D.C.\
 ; Denver\, Colorado\; and Seattle\, Washington. Within academia\, in addi
 tion to UNM\, Villa has taught at Columbia Law School and Seattle Univers
 ity. Among other publications\, Villa is the lead author of ENVIRONMENTAL
  JUSTICE: LAW\, POLICY &amp\; REGULATION (3rd ed. 2020)\, and author of l
 egal scholarship including Law and Lawyers in the Incident Command System
  (2013)\, Remaking Environmental Justice (2020)\; Don't Blame the Flint R
 iver (2022)\, and Environmental Justice Beyond 2 ° (2024). Villa was bor
 n and raised in Albuquerque\, New Mexico\, with local roots tracing back 
 to the Atrisco Land Grant of 1692.Register before 11:59pm on February 22\
 , 2027 to attend.&#160\;
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  This talk is part of Conservation Law C
 enter's "Talking Conservation" lecture series. For more information\, vis
 it www.talkingconservation.org.<br /><br />  Light refreshments will be s
 erved.<br /><br /><span style="text-decoration: underline\;"><strong>Abst
 ract:</strong></span><br />  When activists and scholars speak of "enviro
 nmental justice\," the first word people often hear is race. And for good
  reason – early concerns for environmental justice indeed focused on di
 sproportionate impacts of environmental harm on communities of color and 
 low-income populations. But over time\, environmental justice concerns ha
 ve expanded well beyond race\, to include all people who may be dispropor
 tionately impacted by environmental harm and to consider broader understa
 ndings of harm\, to include cumulative impacts and impacts to cultural an
 d subsistence resources. This presentation will explore what environmenta
 l justice can or should mean today\, against a backdrop of federal retren
 chment\, yet full of hope for the future drawing upon lessons from the pa
 st.<br /><br /><span style="text-decoration: underline\;"><strong>About P
 rofessor Villa:</strong></span><br />  Professor Cliff Villa teaches\, wr
 ites\, and practices in the areas of constitutional rights and environmen
 tal law\, with particular expertise in environmental justice and disaster
  response. On leave from UNM\, Prof. Villa served in the Biden administra
 tion as political leadership for the U.S. EPA Office of Land and Emergenc
 y Management. In that role\, based in Washington\, D.C.\, he provided pol
 icy direction for national programs including Superfund cleanup\, Brownfi
 elds revitalization\, and emergency response. Previously\, Villa served m
 ore than 20 years as an EPA attorney in Washington\, D.C.\; Denver\, Colo
 rado\; and Seattle\, Washington. Within academia\, in addition to UNM\, V
 illa has taught at Columbia Law School and Seattle University. Among othe
 r publications\, Villa is the lead author of ENVIRONMENTAL JUSTICE: LAW\,
  POLICY &amp\; REGULATION (3rd ed. 2020)\, and author of legal scholarshi
 p including Law and Lawyers in the Incident Command System (2013)\, Remak
 ing Environmental Justice (2020)\; Don't Blame the Flint River (2022)\, a
 nd Environmental Justice Beyond 2 ° (2024). Villa was born and raised in
  Albuquerque\, New Mexico\, with local roots tracing back to the Atrisco 
 Land Grant of 1692.</p><p><strong>Register before 11:59pm on February 22\
 , 2027 to attend.&#160\;</strong></p>
UID:20270211T220000Z-2488540@events.iu.edu
DTSTAMP:20261118T150453Z
URL:https://events.iu.edu/lawiub/event/2488540-environmental-justice-past
 -present-and-near-future
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
LAST-MODIFIED:20261120T160329Z
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2488540
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  <strong>Katie Beck<br /></strong>Director
  of Advancement<br />\n  Conservation Law Center<br />\n  admin@conservat
